## Two problems, not one fix

The desk filed date reports together in #4471. Its exported view separates the shared
parser from a misleading booking-form hint. These require different fixes.

<TriageSummary />

## Reports as the desk counts them

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table is the exported desk view. Its “no ticket of its own” label describes that
snapshot; the newly filed issue #12 below supplies the missing ticket. This is not a
live GitHub status view.

Report IDs count once, even when overlapping export pages repeat a row.
Classification uses the tenant directory's locale and booking form, not the reporter's
browser locale, intake tag, or subject. The five reports the desk read as one-day moves
stay filed under the parser cause; they are not a third issue.

## Tickets filed

### Shared parser reads month first

[Issue #11: parser](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/11)

The shared parser treats dates as month-first while the tenant writes day-first.
An ambiguous day/month pair is reversed. Correct the shared parser's date-order contract;
test ambiguous dates against the tenant's expected date order.

### Booking-v3 hint disagrees with the form

[Issue #12: hint](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/12)

Booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. People follow that month-first hint, but the form reads
their input in the tenant's day-first order. Make the visible hint and accepted input
agree. Fixing the shared parser alone does not fix this separate path.

## Engineering disposition — 5 October

After the first accepted submission, the course published an engineering update:
[#4471 closed 05 Oct](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows).

- **Parser — fixed:** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Issue #11 is closed as completed.
- **Hint — won't fix:** booking-v3 form retired. Issue #12 is closed as not planned, not as fixed.

This update is separate from the 4 October export. The table above preserves that
historical snapshot; its open-ticket wording is not the present engineering status.
These outcomes are reported by the course, not fixes implemented by this harness.

## When reports arrived versus when they were merged

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The received and merged bars describe different events. A merge spike does not establish
a new incident or a surge of newly received reports.

## What this changes in the KB

The original incident's “one issue, one fix” claim does not match its own cause table.
The triage rule groups matching subjects, but matching subjects need not mean the same cause,
and different subjects can describe the same cause. Check the tenant and entry path.
The old triage page is stale by the KB's review-age rule; its accepted status is not proof
that its guidance still fits the exported evidence.

This page records findings from the supplied export. It does not silently rewrite
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/), which must remain immutable.
Any replacement decision needs a new numbered record.

## Scope and reproducibility

Counts describe the supplied 2026-10-04 export, not the live support queue.
The desk keeps working after that snapshot. Rebuild the components when data changes;
do not copy stale counts into prose.

Run `npm run check` to validate deduplication and grouping, then `npm run build` to render
the KB. The rendered HTML is committed under `rendered/`.

